Correct: your code correctly interleaves the two halves of the array using push_back, though pre-allocating the vector size would avoid dynamic memory reallocations.
2. REVIEW MY CODE

* What I did well:
* You correctly identified that the first half of the array starts at index 0 and the second half starts at index n.
* You looped n times to pair up nums[i] and nums[i+n] together.
* You returned the final shuffled vector properly.
* Is it correct?
* Yes, it is fully correct and passes all test cases on LeetCode. There are no failing test cases.
* Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses:
* There are no logical bugs or missed edge cases under the problem constraints.
* Starting with an empty vector and calling push_back repeatedly causes multiple dynamic memory reallocations as the vector grows. Pre-allocating vector ans(2 * n) is more efficient.
* Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason:
* Time complexity: O(n) because the loop runs n times doing constant-time operations.
* Space complexity: O(n) auxiliary space because a new vector of size 2n is created to store the answer.
* Why my approach works, or why it doesn't:
* Imagine nums is [2, 5, 1, 3, 4, 7] and n is 3. The first half is [2, 5, 1] and the second half is [3, 4, 7]. When i is 0, you push nums[0] (2) and nums[3] (3). When i is 1, you push nums[1] (5) and nums[4] (4). When i is 2, you push nums[2] (1) and nums[5] (7). This builds [2, 3, 5, 4, 1, 7], which is the exact shuffled order.

3. ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Push Back Simulation (Your Approach)

* Where it comes from:
* Beginner baseline commonly seen in initial attempts on LeetCode.
* Idea:
* Start with an empty vector and push elements from the first half and second half alternatingly using a loop from 0 to n - 1.
* C++ code:

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> shuffle(vector<int>& nums, int n) {
        vector<int> ans;
        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++){
            ans.push_back(nums[i]);
            ans.push_back(nums[i + n]);
        }
        return ans;
    }
};

```

* Time and space complexity:
* Time complexity: O(n) to loop n times.
* Space complexity: O(n) auxiliary space for the output vector.
* What makes the next approach better:
* Starting with an empty vector triggers repeated memory reallocations. Pre-allocating the vector size avoids this overhead.
Approach 2: Pre-allocated Result Vector (Optimal Extra Space)
* Where it comes from:
* LeetCode official editorial and top-voted community solutions.
* Idea:
* Create a result vector of fixed size 2n upfront. Use direct index assignment (ans[2 * i] = nums[i] and ans[2 * i + 1] = nums[i + n]) inside a loop from 0 to n - 1.
* C++ code:

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> shuffle(vector<int>& nums, int n) {
        vector<int> ans(2 * n);
        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++){
            ans[2 * i] = nums[i];
            ans[2 * i + 1] = nums[i + n];
        }
        return ans;
    }
};

```

* Time and space complexity:
* Time complexity: O(n) linear scan.
* Space complexity: O(n) auxiliary space for the output array.
* What makes the next approach better:
* While O(n) extra space is standard, if an interviewer asks for O(1) auxiliary space without allocating a new array, we can pack two numbers into a single integer slot.
Approach 3: In-Place Bit Manipulation (Optimal O(1) Space)
* Where it comes from:
* Advanced interview optimization found in LeetCode discussion forums.
* Idea:
* Since each number in nums is at most 1000, it fits within 10 bits. We can store both x and y in a single 32-bit integer by shifting y left by 10 bits: nums[i] = nums[i] | (nums[i + n] << 10). In a second pass, we unpack them into their correct interleaved positions.
* C++ code:

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> shuffle(vector<int>& nums, int n) {
        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++){
            nums[i] = nums[i] | (nums[i + n] << 10);
        }
        for(int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--){
            nums[2 * i + 1] = nums[i] >> 10;
            nums[2 * i] = nums[i] & 1023;
        }
        return nums;
    }
};

```

* Time and space complexity:
* Time complexity: O(n) linear scans.
* Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space because it modifies the input array in place.

4. WHERE I STAND

* Which approach did I use?
* You used Approach 1 (Push Back Simulation).
* Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem?
* Interviewers expect Approach 2 (Pre-allocated Result Vector) for standard interviews. If it is a strict space constraint interview, they may ask for Approach 3 (In-Place Bit Manipulation).
* Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:
* Question 1: Can you solve this without using extra memory (in-place)?
* Answer: Yes, by packing the numbers into the upper bits of each integer using bitwise OR and left shift, since the values are small enough to fit within 10 bits.
* Question 2: Why is pre-allocating vector ans(2 * n) better than push_back?
* Answer: push_back dynamically grows the vector, doubling its capacity and copying old elements whenever it fills up. Pre-allocating allocates the exact memory block once.

